## Why This Is Asked
Tests depth in diagnosing cross-region tail latency with new failure modes: TLS rotation, DNS dynamics, and QUIC interactions.

## Key Concepts
- QUIC congestion control under multi-tenant edge
- TLS certificate rotation impact on handshakes and 0-RTT
- Edge TLS session tickets and cache behavior
- DNS routing stability and TTL implications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-collection: capture handshake timings between regions
async function recordHandshake(client, server){
  const t0 = performance.now();
  await client.connect(); // initiates TLS over QUIC
  const t1 = performance.now();
  log('handshake', t1 - t0);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate that cert rotation cadence aligns with region clocks without causing handshakes stalls?
- What changes would you make to avoid TLS ticket cache contention in a multi-tenant edge LB?

## Why This Question Is Asked

Diagnosing inter-region TLS latency spikes without code changes tests depth in three critical areas: production observability skills, understanding of certificate handling across geographic boundaries, and knowledge of crypto infrastructure bottlenecks under distributed load.

## Key Concepts

| Concept | Impact |
|---------|--------|
| TLS handshakes | Full handshakes add 1-2 RTTs; resumable sessions reduce this |
| OCSP/CRL fetching | Network path to CA infrastructure adds unpredictable latency |
| Session tickets | Cross-region cache misses force full handshakes |
| ALPN negotiation | Protocol preference mismatch triggers fallbacks |
| HSM signing | Queue buildup during peak loads creates signing stalls |

## Code Example

```javascript
// Measure TLS handshake duration to region endpoints
const tls = require('tls');

function measureHandshake(regionHost) {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    const start = Date.now();
    const socket = tls.connect({
      host: regionHost,
      port: 443,
      servername: regionHost.split('.')[0]
    });

    socket.on('secureConnect', () => {
      const duration = Date.now() - start;
      socket.end();
      resolve({ host: regionHost, duration, sessionReused: socket.isSessionReused() });
    });

    socket.on('error', (err) => resolve({ host: regionHost, error: err.message }));
  });
}

// Execute across all regions
Promise.all([
  measureHandshake('use1.example.com'),
  measureHandshake('usw1.example.com'),
  measureHandshake('euw1.example.com'),
  measureHandshake('apne1.example.com')
]).then(console.log);
```

## Quick Reference: Latency Breakdown

| Scenario | Expected Extra Latency | Key Metric |
|----------|------------------------|------------|
| OCSP stall | 100-500ms | LB debug log timestamps |
| Ticket miss | 1 RTT | Session reuse rate |
| ALPN retry | 1-2 RTT | Protocol negotiated |
| HSM queue | 50-200ms | Signing queue depth |

## Why This Is Asked
Tests hardware offload, OS UDP paths, and QoS interplay in a multi-region fabric, focusing on telemetry reliability under bursts with no app changes.

## Key Concepts
- NIC RSS hashing and RX queues
- UDP tail drops and sockstat counters
- VXLAN/EVPN MTU and fragmentation
- Per-tenant rate limiting and edge QoS

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple UDP burst tester (Node.js)
const dgram = require('dgram');
const client = dgram.createSocket('udp4');
const TARGET = {host: 'collector.example', port: 9000};
let sent = 0, burst = 1000;
function sendNext(){
  if (sent >= burst) { client.close(); return; }
  client.send(Buffer.from('x'), 0, 1, TARGET.port, TARGET.host, () => {});
  sent++;
  if (sent % 10 === 0) setImmediate(sendNext);
  else setTimeout(sendNext, 0);
}
sendNext();
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you differentiate RSS-induced drops from MTU fragmentation issues?
- Which measurements would you automate for a non-disruptive rollback if a mitigation fails?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to diagnose production latency where security tooling (TLS inspection) interacts with edge caches and rate limits.

## Key Concepts

- TLS inspection overhead and queueing
- Firewall connection limits and burst behavior
- Edge CDN caching and TTLs
- Observability: edge logs, pcap, and curl timing data

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple latency probe using fetch
async function probe(url){
  const t0 = performance.now();
  await fetch(url);
  const t1 = performance.now();
  console.log('latency', t1 - t0);
}
probe('https://edge.example/login');
```

